body:
Hi! Using config:
```ts
const config = {
  retries: 4,
  timeout: 'INFINITELY',
  delay: 75,
  // 75 - 150 - 300 - 600
  backoff: (attempt: number, delay: number) => {
    const backoff = delay * 2 ** (attempt - 1);

    console.log('retry backoff used', backoff);

    return backoff;
  },
  logger: (msg: string) => {
    console.warn(getLogTime(), '[retry log]', msg);
  },
}
```

And the sample code:

```ts
try {
  let attempt = 0;

  console.log(getLogTime(), 'before retry');

  await retry(
    async () => {
      console.log('attempt', attempt);
      attempt++;
      throw new Error('sample error message');
    },
    {
      ...commonRetryConfig,
      delay: 75,
      logger: (msg: string) => {
        console.warn(getLogTime(), '[retry log]', msg);
      },
    },
  );

  console.log(getLogTime(), 'after retry');
} catch (e: any) {
  if (e instanceof RetryError) {
    console.error(getLogTime(), 'caught error', e.lastError.message);
  } else if (e instanceof Error) {
    console.error(getLogTime(), 'caught error', e.message);
  } else {
    console.error(getLogTime(), 'caught error', e);
  }
}
```


We can see the result below:

![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/5409131e-e806-4d8b-984b-19684377bf36)

Where we have a timeout between `throw` at last attempt and the moment where the `retry()` actually throws and we can catch an error.

Thanks!


P.S. getLogTime is `const getLogTime = () => format(new Date(), "'['MM/dd/yyyy HH:mm:ss:SSS']'");`
